{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The whole point of a prototype is speed and learning, and the code is expected to be thrown away, so there is no maintenance cost to pay for unread code. Accept whatever runs and keep the feedback loop as tight as possible."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo only needs to work convincingly on the paths you will actually show, so judge AI-written code by behavior and test those paths rather than reading every line. Selective review still matters because a demo that fails live in front of stakeholders is expensive."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Real users will now hit edge cases you did not anticipate, and you will have to change this code soon, so a person should read every change line by line. Speed still matters, but unread code here turns into debt before you have the team or tests to absorb it."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintaining team mean correctness, security, and comprehensibility are ongoing requirements, not one-time checks, so every change gets line-by-line human review. AI can write freely, but the reviewer owns the result."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, accountability, auditability, and reasoning about failure modes matter more than throughput, so a person should write or rewrite every line with AI only suggesting. Even a thorough review at level 2 can miss subtle issues whose cost here is unacceptable."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding is a tool to match to stakes, not a default: use it freely where code is disposable (prototype, level 4), lean on behavior tests and selective review where output is demoed or lightly used (proof-of-concept, level 3), require line-by-line human review once real users and maintenance arrive (mvp and production, level 2), and restrict AI to suggestions where mistakes are costly or regulated (critical, level 1). The common failure mode is treating a low-stakes habit as license to keep it as stakes rise; re-evaluate the level every time the audience or consequences of the software change."
}
